96	<div class="wrap">
97	  <header class="head">
98	    <div class="eyebrow">Code audit before back-office rollout</div>
99	    <h1>Saleor Back-Office Problem Register</h1>
100	    <p class="lede">Problems found in the Saleor code that staff or the application can run into once the back office is in daily use. Each entry says what goes wrong, when, and what it costs. No fixes are proposed.</p>
101	    <div class="meta">
102	      <span>commit b8025bac2a (upstream main, 3.23 dev)</span>
103	      <span>24 Nov 2026</span>
104	      <span>read-only, no code changed</span>
105	    </div>
106	  </header>
107	
108	  <section>
109	    <h2>Count by area and severity</h2>
110	    <div class="panel scroll"><table id="matrix"></table></div>
111	    <p class="note"><strong>Critical</strong>: silent loss or corruption of money, stock or order data from ordinary staff use or one settings change. <strong>High</strong>: wrong numbers, duplicated side effects or stuck operations that need a double-click, two people on one record, or an outage. <strong>Medium</strong>: needs unusual timing or configuration, or is a misleading error or limitation with a workaround. <strong>Low</strong>: narrow, cosmetic, or only at scale.</p>
112	  </section>
113	
114	  <section>
115	    <h2>Read these first</h2>
116	    <p class="note">The entries most likely to cost money or corrupt records in the first weeks.</p>
117	    <div class="panel"><ol class="short" id="short"></ol></div>
118	  </section>
119	
120	  <section>
121	    <h2>The register</h2>
122	    <div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter entries">
123	      <button id="f-all" data-sev="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button>
124	      <button id="f-critical" data-sev="critical" aria-pressed="false">Critical</button>
125	      <button id="f-high" data-sev="high" aria-pressed="false">High</button>
126	      <button id="f-medium" data-sev="medium" aria-pressed="false">Medium</button>
127	      <button id="f-low" data-sev="low" aria-pressed="false">Low</button>
128	      <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
129	      <button id="f-chk" aria-pressed="false">Re-checked only</button>
130	    </div>
131	    <p class="note"><strong>Re-checked</strong> means the cited code was read a second time and supports the entry. <strong>Single pass</strong> means one audit pass traced it and it was not read again. Line numbers refer to the commit above.</p>
132	    <nav class="areanav" id="areanav" aria-label="Areas"></nav>
133	  </section>
134	
135	  <div id="areas" style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:40px;"></div>
136	
137	  <section>
138	    <h2>Patterns behind the entries</h2>
139	    <p class="note">The same few causes account for most of the register.</p>
140	    <div class="cols">
141	      <div><h3>Checked before the lock, written after</h3>
142	        <p>A value is validated on a copy loaded earlier and then written back. A double click or two staff on one record is enough to trigger it.</p>
143	        <p class="ref">FU-1, FU-3, FU-5, DO-4, DO-5, RR-2, TX-1, TX-5, ST-9</p></div>
144	      <div><h3>Read from the replica, written to the primary</h3>
145	        <p>Periodic tasks pick their work from the read replica and act on the primary without re-checking. Lag or an overlapping run repeats or misapplies the action.</p>
146	        <p class="ref">BT-2, BT-3, BT-4, BT-7, TX-14, WH-9</p></div>
147	      <div><h3>Zero treated as 'not given'</h3>
148	        <p>An amount of 0 falls through to a default, which in the refund paths is the full charged amount.</p>
149	        <p class="ref">RR-1, TX-7, DO-13</p></div>
150	      <div><h3>Ids not checked against their parent</h3>
151	        <p>A mutation takes an order plus line or transaction ids and never checks that they belong together, or that the staff member may act in that channel.</p>
152	        <p class="ref">FU-2, RR-3, RR-11, RR-12, TX-6</p></div>
153	      <div><h3>App failures that look like normal results</h3>
154	        <p>When a tax, shipping or payment app is slow or down, Saleor carries on with untaxed totals, unfiltered shipping methods, or a recorded 'failure' for an action that may have happened.</p>
155	        <p class="ref">DO-6, WH-6, TX-4, WH-7</p></div>
156	      <div><h3>States with no way out</h3>
157	        <p>Several records can end up pending with no retry, edit or delete available to staff, and nothing that sweeps them up.</p>
158	        <p class="ref">RR-6, TX-3, BT-5, BT-6, WH-2, WH-10</p></div>
159	    </div>
160	  </section>
161	
162	  <section>
163	    <h2>Checked and found sound</h2>
164	    <ul class="plain">
165	      <li>Order, fulfillment, payment and gift-card webhooks and emails are sent only after the database commit, so nothing goes out for a change that was rolled back.</li>
166	      <li>Fulfilling the full remaining quantity twice is rejected. Only overlapping partial fulfillments get through (FU-1).</li>
167	      <li>Refunding more than was captured on a legacy payment is blocked under a lock. Repeating a refund one after the other is blocked; only simultaneous requests get through (RR-2).</li>
168	      <li>Duplicate payment events that carry a payment reference are detected under a lock, and order totals from transactions are recomputed under order and transaction locks.</li>
169	      <li>One payment app cannot modify another app's transaction.</li>
170	      <li>Money is handled as decimals throughout, and currencies are validated against the order or checkout.</li>
171	      <li>No background task deletes or expires draft orders. Insufficient stock at draft completion rolls everything back and names the line.</li>
172	      <li>Cancelling or expiring an order twice does not release stock twice. Expired stock reservations never block staff.</li>
173	      <li>Cleanup of expired checkouts and of old webhook payloads runs in bounded batches. Webhook targets are never followed through redirects, calls have explicit timeouts, and signatures are applied.</li>
174	      <li>The fulfillment and refund-reason mutations read site settings per request, not from the per-process cache.</li>
175	    </ul>
176	  </section>
177	
178	  <section>
179	    <h2>How this was done, and its limits</h2>
180	    <ul class="plain">
181	      <li>The code is unmodified upstream Saleor at the commit above. These are properties of stock Saleor, not of local changes.</li>
182	      <li>Everything comes from reading code. No database was available, so the test suite was not run and no race was reproduced. The one exception is ST-1, where the underlying Django behaviour was run and confirmed.</li>
183	      <li>46 of the 94 entries were read a second time against the cited code. The other 48 were traced once and are marked 'Single pass'. Treat those as leads to confirm before acting on them.</li>
184	      <li>The dashboard is not in this repository. Some entries may be unreachable from its screens and reachable only by integrations or direct API calls. Where an entry needs unusual input, its 'When' line says so.</li>
185	      <li>Several entries depend on configuration: stock reservations, manual fulfillment approval, 'include draft orders in voucher usage', order expiry, legacy payments versus the Transactions API, and whether a read replica and private storage bucket are in use. Entries that apply only under one setting say which.</li>
186	      <li>Timing-dependent entries state the window. Narrow windows become likely under double clicks, retries by integrations, and queue backlogs.</li>
187	    </ul>
188	  </section>
